\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a finite group $G$, $\operatorname{Aut}(G)$ is the group of all bijective homomorphisms from $G$ to itself. Its order and $|G|$ are positive integers. No commutativity, simplicity, or restriction on the order of $G$ is imposed.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is
\[
 \left\{\frac{|\operatorname{Aut}(G)|}{|G|}:G\text{ is a finite group}\right\}
 =\mathbb Q_{>0}?
\]
Equivalently, for every positive rational $r$, does some finite group satisfy $|\operatorname{Aut}(G)|/|G|=r$ exactly? Density of this set in the nonnegative real numbers would be a weaker assertion. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every positive rational occur exactly as a finite group’s automorphism-group order divided by its own order?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2606 (KOU-21.97). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] M. Tărnăuceanu and R. Molinier, Problem 21.97, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records an affirmative answer; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\end{description}
